# Documentation

Three linters split the work and never overlap: **markdownlint** owns the document structure,
**textlint** the prose, **cspell** the individual words. All three run in `pnpm lint`;
`pnpm fix` autofixes markdownlint and textlint.

`packages/oas-truth/README.md` is the user documentation — installation, quick start, what
`parseOpenAPI` does — and it is what npm shows. The root `README.md` is a **symlink** to it, so
GitHub shows the same file. Edit the package copy; never replace the symlink with a file (npm
leaves symlinks out of the tarball, which is why the real file lives in the package). Links
inside it are absolute `https://github.com/...` URLs, because a relative link breaks on npm.

## Structure (markdownlint)

- Every default rule is on. A rule that does not fit is turned off in
  `.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc` with the reason beside it — never left silently unset.
- The first line is the top-level heading.
- Duplicate headings are allowed only under different parents (`MD024` is `siblings_only`).
- **Every fenced block declares a language, from the closed list**: `ts`, `js`, `json`, `jsonc`,
  `yaml`, `sh`, `text`, `diff`, `tsp`. Terminal output and directory trees are `text`.
  `typescript` is not in the list on purpose — one spelling per language.
- Line length is unchecked (a table row cannot wrap).

## Prose (textlint)

- The docs are English. Do not add Japanese prose to a `.md` file.
- `terminology` enforces the canonical spelling of technical terms: **JavaScript**,
  **TypeScript**, **npm**, **GitHub**, **JSON**, **OpenAPI** (never "Open API"), **TypeSpec**.
  Code spans and fenced blocks are skipped.
- `README` and `re-export` are excluded from the term list on purpose.
- Quotes, brackets and parentheses must balance (`no-unmatched-pair`).
- No `TODO` in the docs.
- A deliberate exception is a `<!-- textlint-disable rule-name -->` comment next to the sentence
  that needs it, never a change to `.textlintrc.json`.

## Spelling (cspell)

- cspell reads **every** tracked file — source, tests, configs, workflows, these rules — not just
  the docs, in **US English** (`behavior`, `normalize`).
- A genuine project term (a package, tool or API name that recurs) is a one-line addition to
  `.cspell/words.txt`, kept sorted. Never add a typo there, and never add `ignoreWords` or
  `ignorePaths` to `cspell.config.jsonc` to silence a word.
- Deliberate non-words in test data (another language, an encoded identifier under test) are
  declared in that file only: `// cspell:ignore Cafue`, with a comment saying why.
- camelCase and snake_case are split automatically, and everything git ignores is skipped.
